Z||000002||thomas_l||11-30-2001||08:50 AM||||Those guys ran till now (versus Napster) because they don't maintain an index of downloadables. In other words, they have zero control over what is traded peer to peer.<P>The outcome of this case will be very interesting with respect to the liability of "enablers" of an illegal act. Next they will go after the manufacturers of CD-RW drives and the computers they are attached to because they are enabling copyright infringement. While they're at it, they oughtta shut down everybody. This scotch tape dispenser could enable me to tape someone to death  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||11-30-2001||08:55 AM||||I wonder how they got around the Video Tape precedent...<P>Video producers tried to have VCR's outlawed or controlled because you could make an illegal copy of a copywrited video.  That was defeated and the reason was that it's not sufficient for the technology to "enable" an illegal act.  You actually had to prosecute for the actual illegal act. <P>Napster tried to use this argument to stay afloat, but to no avail.  They were maintaining it was the same.  It wasn't there responsibility that they enabled illegal file sharing.  It was the people doing the actual sharing of copywrited files where were committing a crime.  I tend to agree.||||reg||1
